    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A duchy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> centred on Leuven, Brussels and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#antwerp">Antwerp</a>, among the most urbanised lands of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>.</li><li>Its Joyous Entry of 1356 bound every new duke to respect the estates' rights and was sworn by Burgundian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rulers alike.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#antwerp">Antwerp</a> made it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>'s commercial hub in the 1500s; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> split it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> annexed the rest in 1795.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-i-duke-of-brabant">John I of Brabant</a>, victor at Worringen in 1288, was also a poet: love songs under his name survive in the famous Codex Manesse of German lyric poetry.</p></aside>
    </section>

